,"choice1":" ","choice2":" ","choice3":" ","choice4":" ","choice5":" ","freeAnswerChoice":" ","explanation":"<p>The notation $a \\not\\mid b$ means $a$ does not divides $b.$ So, $3 \\not\\mid 5$ means $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{$3$ doesn't divide $5$}}.$</p>\n<p>According to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ does not divide $b$ means that for any integer $k,$ we have that \n\\[\nb \\neq a k.\n\\] </p>\n<p>Therefore, $3 \\not\\mid 5$ is equivalent to the statement that $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{for any}}$ $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{integer}}$ $k,$ we have $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}5 \\neq 3k}.$</p>\n","topic":{"id":3080,"name":"Integer Divisibility"},"example":{"id":14982,"title":"Stating the Definition of Non-Divisibility"},"group":{"id":33137,"calculator":"None"},"graphics":[],"selects":[],"inputAnswers":[],"body":{"segments":[{"newParagraph":true},{"text":"The notation $3 \\not\\mid 5$ means "},{"select":{"options":["$3$ doesn't divide $5$","$3$ divides $5$","$5$ doesn't divide $3$","$5$ divides $3$"],"margin":{"left":4,"right":2,"top":0,"bottom":2},"context":"text","index":0,"selectedIndex":0,"correct":true,"disabled":true}},{"text":". It is equivalent to the statement that "},{"select":{"options":["for any","for some"],"margin":{"left":4,"right":4,"top":0,"bottom":2},"context":"text","index":1,"selectedIndex":0,"correct":true,"disabled":true}},{"select":{"options":["integer","number"],"margin":{"left":4,"right":4,"top":0,"bottom":2},"context":"text","index":2,"selectedIndex":0,"correct":true,"disabled":true}},{"text":" $k,$ we have "},{"select":{"options":["$5 \\neq 3k$","$3 \\neq 5k$"],"margin":{"left":4,"right":2,"top":0,"bottom":2},"context":"text","index":3,"selectedIndex":0,"correct":true,"disabled":true}},{"text":"."}]},"taskId":14272325,"answer":{"id":62403236,"cread":"2026-10-16T19:48:22.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":null,"freeResponse":"[0,0,0,0]","timeElapsed":22665},"graphic":false,"difficultyClass":"difficultyEasy","difficultySymbol":"E","rendered":true,"testMode":false}},{"id":62403249,"created":"2026-10-16T19:48:38.000Z","choices":null,"correct":0,"selectedChoice":5,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":12154,"number":4,"createdStr":"Fri, Oct 16th @ 7:48 PM","timeElapsedFormatted":"0:12","question":{"id":221922,"format":"MC","difficulty":1,"exampleId":14983,"text":"<p>Which of the following statements must be true for any integers $x$ and $y?$</p>\n<ol class=\"questionStatements\"><li>  $2$ divides $(12x+21y)$ </li><li>  $3$ divides $(12x+21y)$ </li><li>  $4$ divides $(12x+21y)$ </li></ol>","choice1":"II only","choice2":"II and III only","choice3":"III only","choice4":"I only","choice5":"I and II only","freeAnswerChoice":" ","explanation":"<p>The notation $a \\mid b$ means $a$ divides $b.$</p>\n<p>According to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\\[\nb=a k.\n\\] \nWith that in mind, let&#39;s examine our statement in turn.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Statement I is false. For example, if $x=0$ and $y=1,$ we have $12(0)+21(1) = 21,$ which is not divisible by $2.$</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Statement II is true. Notice that \n\\[\n12x+21y = 3(4x+7y).\n\\]\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $4x+7y$ must be an integer, too. So, $3$ divides $(12x+21y).$</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Statement III is false. For example, if $x=0$ and $y=1,$ we have $12(0)+21(1) = 21,$ which is not divisible by $4.$</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Therefore, the correct answer is &quot;II only.&quot;</p>\n","topic":{"id":3080,"name":"Integer Divisibility"},"example":{"id":14983,"title":"Inferring Divisibility in Algebraic Expressions  "},"group":{"id":33138,"calculator":"None"},"graphics":[],"selects":[],"inputAnswers":[],"body":null,"taskId":14272325,"answer":{"id":62403249,"cread":"2026-10-16T19:48:38.000Z","choices":null,"correct":0,"selectedChoice":5,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":12154},"graphic":false,"difficultyClass":"difficultyEasy","difficultySymbol":"E","rendered":true,"testMode":false}},{"id":62403264,"created":"2026-10-16T19:48:58.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":1,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":15205,"number":5,"createdStr":"Fri, Oct 16th @ 7:48 PM","timeElapsedFormatted":"0:15","question":{"id":221926,"format":"MC","difficulty":1,"exampleId":14983,"text":"<p>Which of the following statements must be true for any integers $m$ and $n?$</p>\n<ol class=\"questionStatements\"><li>  $5 \\mid (5m+10n)$ </li><li>  $3 \\mid (5m+10n)$ </li><li>  $2 \\mid (5m+10n)$ </li></ol>","choice1":"I  only","choice2":" III only","choice3":"I, II and III","choice4":"II only","choice5":"I and III only","freeAnswerChoice":" ","explanation":"<p>The notation $a \\mid b$ means $a$ divides $b.$</p>\n<p>According to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\\[\nb=a k.\n\\] \nWith that in mind, let&#39;s examine our statement in turn.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Statement I is true. Notice that \n\\[\n5m+10n= 5(m+2n).\n\\]\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $m+2n$ must be an integer, too. So, $5 \\mid (5m+10n).$</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Statement II is false. For example, if $m=1$ and $n=0,$ we have $5(1)+10(0) = 5,$ which is not divisible by $3.$</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Statement III is false. For example, if $m=1$ and $n=0,$ we have $5(1)+10(0) = 5,$ which is not divisible by $2.$</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Therefore, the correct answer is &quot;I only.&quot;</p>\n","topic":{"id":3080,"name":"Integer Divisibility"},"example":{"id":14983,"title":"Inferring Divisibility in Algebraic Expressions  "},"group":{"id":33138,"calculator":"None"},"graphics":[],"selects":[],"inputAnswers":[],"body":null,"taskId":14272325,"answer":{"id":62403264,"cread":"2026-10-16T19:48:58.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":1,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":15205},"graphic":false,"difficultyClass":"